# Migration Data API grants: explicit, always

Since `20260820100400_revoke_default_data_api_grants.sql`, objects created in
`public` get NO automatic Data API grants. This repo opted in early to the
Supabase platform default that reaches every existing project on October 30,
2026 (changelog 45329). supabase-js / PostgREST cannot see a new table, view,
sequence, or function, EVEN WITH THE SERVICE-ROLE KEY, until the migration
grants access explicitly. `supabase db push` applies a grant-less migration
cleanly; the breakage only shows up at runtime as "permission denied", so
grants are not optional polish, they are part of creating the object.

Every migration that creates an object must grant in the SAME file:

```sql
-- Service-role-only table (the default posture: RLS on, zero policies)
create table public.example (...);
alter table public.example enable row level security;
grant select, insert, update, delete on table public.example to service_role;

-- Serial column? Grant its sequence too (or prefer an identity column,
-- which needs no sequence grant)
grant usage, select on sequence public.example_id_seq to service_role;

-- View read by the app
grant select on public.example_view to service_role;

-- Function called via .rpc() (trigger / event-trigger functions need no
-- grant: they run as owner and are never called through PostgREST)
grant execute on function public.example_fn(uuid) to service_role;
```

Rules:

1. Grant `authenticated` / `anon` ONLY when the table is deliberately
   client-readable through RLS policies (the notifications / dashboard-chat /
   ai_flow_library pattern). Everything else is service_role-only.
2. The `fn_grants_lockdown` event trigger still strips anon / authenticated /
   PUBLIC EXECUTE from every new function; that guard is unchanged. What is
   new is that service_role EXECUTE no longer appears by default.
3. Deliberately unexposed object (e.g. a pg_cron-only helper that must never
   be a PostgREST RPC)? Add a marker comment naming it:
   `-- grants: none (my_helper): pg_cron runs it as owner`.
4. Never re-enable the auto-expose default (dashboard "Automatically expose
   new tables and functions" toggle); deny-by-default is the documented
   security posture.

CI enforces this: `tests/migration-grants.test.ts` fails any migration
stamped after the convention start that creates an object without a matching
service_role grant or marker, and the `Worker Integration (local Supabase)`
job runs the migrations against a stack with the same revoked defaults, so a
missed grant fails the PR instead of production.
